1: # Selected-cusp double source loop: actual trace and line comparison
2: 
3: Working proof submitted for audit as P3F.56. This file concerns only the
4: twofold marked closure of P3F.54, with the III-star cap and no logarithmic
5: disc on either member of the coalescing finite-node pair. The source scalar
6: is fixed and sufficiently small. Use the standard vertical-edge fan of F41,
7: whose physical circle has the fixed sphere used below. The value for another
8: fan is not inferred without its actual framing comparison. This proof does
9: not replace this source loop by
10: a scalar loop. The local lifted-link invariant is the separately computed
11: P3F.55; the purpose here is its actual geometric transfer.
12: 
13: ## SC.1. The exact normalization and the ordered critical-value braid
14: 
15: Retain the F54 functions and put
16: \[
17:  a(u)=u+u^{-2}=\rho^2,\qquad u(0)=u_*,\quad u_*^3=-1.
18: \]
19: The selected fibre is at \(q=t-t_0(u)=0\). In the actual source-parameter
20: threefold, the coordinates \((X,y,\rho)\) are smooth coordinates and
21: \[
22:  q=\frac{y^2-X^2(X+\rho^2)}{X+u(\rho)}.                 \tag{SC.1}
23: \]
24: The entire selected rational fibre is normalized by
25: \[
26:  f(s,\rho)=\bigl(s^2-\rho^2,
27:                     s(s^2-\rho^2),\rho\bigr).         \tag{SC.2}
28: \]
29: At \(s=\infty\) this is the zero-section point, so its domain is
30: \(N=\mathbf P^1_s\times D_\rho\). Its two preimages of the node are
31: the actual sections
32: \[
33:  D_+=\{s=\rho\},\qquad D_-=\{s=-\rho\}.              \tag{SC.3}
34: \]
35: They meet only at \((s,\rho)=(0,0)\).
36: 
37: The derivative minors of (SC.2) are
38: \[
39:  2s,\qquad 3s^2-\rho^2,\qquad
40:                       2\rho(s^2-\rho^2).
41: \]
42: Their ideal is \((s,\rho^2)\). Thus \(f\) is a complex immersion off
43: the origin. Its only distinct double pairs there are
44: \((\rho,\rho)\) and \((-\rho,\rho)\): equality of the last and first
45: coordinates first gives the same \(\rho\) and \(s'=\pm s\), and equality
46: of the second then gives \(s(s^2-\rho^2)=0\).
47: 
48: The two nearby critical values of (SC.1) can also be read without a
49: change of the base coordinate. Besides \(X=0,y=0\), the second critical
50: point is \(X=aR(a),y=0\), where
51: \[
52:  2aR^2+(a+3u)R+2u=0,\qquad R(0)=-2/3.
53: \]
54: The implicit derivative in \(R\) is \(3u_*\ne0\). Its actual critical
55: value is
56: \[
57:  q_1(\rho)
58:    =-\rho^6\frac{R(\rho^2)^2(R(\rho^2)+1)}
59:                     {\rho^2R(\rho^2)+u(\rho)},\qquad
60:  \frac{q_1(\rho)}{\rho^6}\longrightarrow-\frac4{27u_*}.
61:                                                                \tag{SC.4}
62: \]
63: The remaining critical point stays outside this small source chart.
64: For one positive \(\rho\)-loop the ordered pair \((0,q_1)\) therefore
65: has six positive full turns of its difference. In the convention that
66: the positive generator \(\sigma\) of the two-point braid group is a
67: positive half turn, this is exactly \(\sigma^{12}\).
68: 
69: Here is the retained Hurwitz convention. For an ordered pair of positive
70: Picard--Lefschetz twists \((g,h)\), the right Hurwitz action is
71: \[
72:  (g,h)\cdot\sigma=(h,h^{-1}gh).
73: \]
74: Writing \(C=gh\), direct multiplication gives
75: \[
76:  (g,h)\cdot\sigma^{2j}
77:        =(C^{-j}gC^j,C^{-j}hC^j).                    \tag{SC.5}
78: \]
79: The two vanishing arcs are adjacent arcs between the three roots of the
80: cubic in (SC.1). Their lifts to its double cover meet once. The local
81: regular fibre is the double cover of a disc branched at these three
82: points, hence a once-punctured torus. The positive twists are the lifts
83: of the two positive adjacent half twists. The braid
84: \((\sigma_1\sigma_2)^3\) is one full turn of the three branch points.
85: Its lift rotates the inner boundary coordinate by half a turn, because
86: the boundary covering has degree two. Squaring it gives the actual
87: boundary twist:
88: \[
89:                         (gh)^6=T_{\partial}.        \tag{SC.6}
90: \]
91: For example, on a boundary collar with covering angle
92: \(\vartheta=2\widetilde\vartheta\), the base twist
93: \(\vartheta\mapsto\vartheta+2\pi\chi(r)\) lifts to
94: \(\widetilde\vartheta\mapsto\widetilde\vartheta+\pi\chi(r)\);
95: its square is the positive boundary twist of the lifted collar.
96: This is the boundary map, not merely the order-six homology matrix.
97: Thus (SC.5) retains conjugation by \(T_{\partial}^{-1}\) after the
98: positive \(\rho\)-loop. The ordered vanishing curves return because the
99: boundary twist is central, while the boundary twist itself is retained.
100: The weighted chart rotates the three cubic roots twice and has the
101: opposite, inner-to-outer boundary arrow \(T_{\partial}\). These two
102: arrows are the same gluing relation read in opposite directions.
103: 
104: ## SC.2. The explicit compact pencil and its plumbing
105: 
106: The substitution
107: \[
108:  X=\rho^2\widetilde X,\qquad
109:  y=\rho^3\widetilde Y,\qquad q=\rho^6Q
110: \]
111: is the \(\rho\)-chart of the weighted blowup of the smooth coordinates
112: \((X,y,\rho)\) with weights \((2,3,1)\). The complete equation is
113: \[
114:  \widetilde Y^2=\widetilde X^2(\widetilde X+1)
115:                     +Q\bigl(u(\rho)+\rho^2\widetilde X\bigr).
116:                                                                \tag{SC.7}
117: \]
118: On the exceptional weighted plane \(\mathbf P(2,3,1)\), with coordinates
119: \([X:Y:R]\), the actual pencil is
120: \[
121:  Q=[Y^2-X^2(X+R^2):u_*R^6].                          \tag{SC.8}
122: \]
123: The constant base unit \(u_*\) is retained.
124: 
125: Its only base point is the smooth point \([1:1:0]\). In local coordinates
126: \(R,z=Y^2-X^2(X+R^2)\), the ideal is \((z,R^6)\). The six successive
127: blowups with charts \(z=R^jz_j\), \(j=1,\ldots,6\), resolve the
128: pencil. The first five exceptional curves are in the infinite fibre,
129: with multiplicities \(5,4,3,2,1\); the sixth is its section. The proper
130: curve \(R=0\) has multiplicity six. The quotient point of order two on
131: this curve has the scalar \((1,1)\) chart; its resolution contributes a
132: single curve with multiplicity three. The order-three quotient has
133: weights \((2,1)\); the two regular rays have \(R\)-valuations
134: \(2/3,1/3\), and contribute multiplicities four and two, in that order
135: away from the central curve.
136: 
137: Every vertical curve has self-intersection \(-2\). For the central
138: curve this can also be checked before the point blowups: its weighted
139: self-intersection \(1/6\), minus the two resolution corrections
140: \(1/2\) and \(2/3\), is \(-1\); the first ordinary base-point blowup
141: makes it \(-2\). Each of the first five ordinary exceptional curves
142: is blown up once more, while the last section remains a \((-1)\)-curve.
143: Thus the infinite fibre is the literal affine \(E_8\) plumbing with
144: central multiplicity six and three arms
145: \[
146:  (5,4,3,2,1),\qquad(3),\qquad(4,2).                  \tag{SC.9}
147: \]
148: The finite discriminant of (SC.7) at \(\rho=0\) is
149: \(-u_*Q(4+27u_*Q)\), giving the two displayed nodal fibres. The compact
150: source pencil therefore has \(II^*+I_1+I_1\). Its integral intersection
151: matrix, including the last section, is unimodular of signature \(-8\);
152: the executed certificate supplies the integral basis consisting of the
153: fibre, section and the eight finite \(E_8\) roots.
154: 
155: The pullback of the original source Cartier line to this blowup and all
156: six further blowups is trivial near the exceptional pencil: both original
157: divisor sections avoid the source cusp point. This assertion is about
158: that literal pullback. It is not a substitution of the compact pencil
159: for the actual principal-circle line. The latter comparison is supplied
160: by the real trace below.
161: 
162: ## SC.3. The actual ambient orbit product and the embedded trace
163: 
164: Choose a small base disc containing just the two coalescing finite
165: critical values, and a small source-parameter disc. On its boundary the
166: elliptic map is a submersion for every parameter. The source total family
167: is smooth, including the cuspidal member, and is proper with this boundary.
168: A horizontal field tangent to the boundary therefore gives a smooth
169: source trivialization over the parameter disc. The III-star cap and the
170: ordinary complement extend there by the actual full-coefficient maps of
171: F51/F54. These choices retain their original annular boundary marking.
172: 
173: The suspension translation also has a logarithm on this small base and
174: parameter polydisc. At the central cuspidal fibre its smooth group is
175: additive. Divide its central section value by a sufficiently large
176: integer, continue that division section by the inverse function theorem,
177: and take the small-group logarithm of this section. Multiplication by the
178: same integer gives a logarithm of the actual section. Exponentiating
179: \(t\) times it, \(0\leq t\leq1\), supplies an actual extending
180: translation isotopy. The invertible line lift obtained after dividing
181: the chosen Hom section by its base factor \(q\) has the same construction
182: in the line-extension group: its scalar unit has an analytic logarithm
183: on the polydisc. Thus this isotopy retains the source-line lift as well
184: as the source section.
185: 
186: Use this isotopy to untwist the source suspension. At the selected node
187: use the exact relative orbit product of P3F.9. Its local formulas
188: \[
189:  2b=r-|y_0|^2/r,\qquad
190:  2b'=|x_0|^2r-r^{-1},\qquad db'/db>0
191: \]
192: give a proper increasing gamma coordinate, fixed on the prescribed
193: outside collar. They identify the actual orbit body with the source
194: neighbourhood times its marked gamma circle. They are periodic on the
195: \(\rho\)-cover, since the ordered node coordinates return literally
196: by F54. The target source product exists also at \(\rho=0\). Gluing it
197: to the already extending exterior gives a smooth ambient orbit-body
198: family over the disc. We use its trivialization as \(Y\times D_\rho\).
199: No extension of the complex threefold at \(\rho=0\) is made here.
200: 
201: The source projection of the fixed sphere is the actual normalization
202: (SC.2). Fractional source translations used in the suspension product
203: do not change this local normalization germ. Here is their exact
204: normalization map. If \(Z=(s-\rho)/(s+\rho)\) and a translation has
205: Tate multiplier \(g(\rho)\), with \(g(0)=1\) and
206: \((1-g)/\rho\) analytic, then
207: \[
208:  s\longmapsto
209:  \frac{(1+g)s+(1-g)\rho}
210:       {((1-g)/\rho)s+(1+g)}.                        \tag{SC.10}
211: \]
212: It is analytic at the origin, fixes \(s=\pm\rho\), and has invertible
213: derivative there. The same formula for the fractional multiplier
214: \(\exp(t\log g)\) provides the actual isotopy. The positive product
215: coordinate may be chosen in an arbitrarily short gamma arc. Its
216: source shear is then isotopic to this normalization map through
217: invertible derivatives; this is the same relative positive-coordinate
218: comparison used in P3F.9 and K30.
219: 
220: The order of the two gamma heights is fixed by the charts, rather than
221: chosen afterward. The source branch \(y_0=0\) is \(s=-\rho\) and
222: occurs at the lower endpoint \(b=0\). The branch \(x_0=0\) is
223: \(s=+\rho\) and occurs at the upper endpoint \(b'=0\). The increasing
224: overlap identifies them in precisely this order.
225: 
226: On the local normalization put
227: \[
228:  H(s,\rho)=\frac{\operatorname{Re}(s\overline\rho)}
229:                   {\sqrt{|s|^2+|\rho|^2}}.          \tag{SC.11}
230: \]
231: It has the same strict ordering on the two branches. Multiplying by a
232: fixed small positive constant puts it in the chosen gamma arc. Extend
233: it with a cutoff over the rest of \(\mathbf P^1\). On a collar of the
234: outer parameter circle retain the actual gamma graph. Interpolate by
235: a convex combination on the intervening annulus. The difference of
236: the heights on every double pair remains positive. Away from those
237: pairs the source map is already injective. Hence this is an isotopy
238: through embedded graphs, with the actual outer trace unchanged.
239: 
240: Remove a small complex four-ball around the origin from \(N\), and call
241: the result \(W\). The map \((f,H)\), with the just specified outer
242: interpolation, is an actual smooth embedding of \(W\) in
243: \(Y\times D_\rho\), and its outer boundary is the actual fixed-sphere
244: trace with orientation \(F\times S^1_\rho\). Its inner boundary is the
245: lifted link of (SC.2).
246: 
247: For precision, this link can be put in the ordinary six-sphere without
248: assuming radial injectivity. The map \((f,H)\) is homogeneous for the
249: positive weighted target action of weights \((2,3,1,1)\) on
250: \((X,y,\rho,\gamma)\). The squared Euclidean norm is strictly
251: increasing along every nonzero such orbit. Weighted radial projection
252: therefore gives a smooth embedding of the domain three-sphere in the
253: ordinary \(S^6\). Injectivity follows from the double-pair calculation;
254: transversality follows because the weighted radial vector is the image
255: of the domain radial vector. This is the exact lifted link used in F55.
256: 
257: ## SC.4. The extending normal spinor and actual Hopf circle line
258: 
259: The finite-base source has a nowhere-zero holomorphic two-form. In the
260: coordinates (SC.1), its residue expression is a nonzero holomorphic
261: unit times \(dX\wedge dy\). Together with \(d\rho\) it trivializes the
262: ambient complex determinant along the normalization. The complex normal
263: line of the immersion (SC.2) on \(W\) is consequently
264: \[
265:  L=N_f\cong\mathcal O(-D_+-D_-).                     \tag{SC.12}
266: \]
267: This is a determinant isomorphism, not merely a degree equality. In the
268: finite coordinate chart its normal covector is the explicit cross product
269: \[
270:  (3s^2-\rho^2)\,dX-2s\,dy
271:                     +2\rho(s^2-\rho^2)\,d\rho.     \tag{SC.13}
272: \]
273: It is nonzero everywhere on \(W\) in this chart. Changing to the
274: infinite normalization coordinate gives the \(\mathcal O(-2)\)
275: normal transition. The divisor identity
276: \(\operatorname{div}(s^2-\rho^2)=D_++D_--2\{s=\infty\}\)
277: gives the displayed actual isomorphism (SC.12).
278: 
279: The normal bundle of the real graph is isomorphic to
280: \(L_{\mathbb R}\oplus\mathbb R_\gamma\): quotient by its tangent
281: graph gives the identity on the source normal two-plane and the gamma
282: coordinate, with only a triangular shear. Its linear contraction is
283: the precise graph-normal comparison. Retain the same constant orientation
284: change as in the positive-Hopf convention of K7.
285: 
286: Define the rank-two complex spinor bundle
287: \[
288:  E=(\mathcal O\oplus L)\otimes\mathcal O(D_+)
289:        =\mathcal O(D_+)\oplus\mathcal O(-D_-).        \tag{SC.14}
290: \]
291: Its determinant is \(\mathcal O(D_+-D_-)\), trivialized by the literal
292: Cartier ratio \((s-\rho)/(s+\rho)\). Thus \(E\) is an actual
293: \(SU(2)\) bundle, and
294: \[
295:  \operatorname{ad}E\cong
296:               \mathbb R\oplus L_{\mathbb R}.        \tag{SC.15}
297: \]
298: The off-diagonal summand is exactly
299: \(\operatorname{Hom}(\mathcal O(D_+),\mathcal O(-D_-))=L\).
300: Its Hopf sphere bundle carries the meridian line with determinant
301: class \(b=0\).
302: 
303: This is the actual outer circle line. In the normalization coordinate
304: \(Z=(s-\rho)/(s+\rho)\), the two Cox normal coordinates have transition
305: \(c_i'=Zc_i\) and physical weights \((-1,+1)\). Conjugating the
306: negative-weight coordinate gives the unitary transition
307: \[
308:        \operatorname{diag}(\overline Z/|Z|,Z/|Z|),
309: \]
310: which is precisely that of (SC.14), after the explicit Hermitian
311: identification of the conjugate tautological line with its dual. It
312: intertwines the physical circle actions. The quaternion formula
313: \[
314:  q_{\rm H}=(1+jZ)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})
315: \]
316: is the same formula as K30; its second-chart expression gives the
317: identical vector. The ratio \([s-\rho:s+\rho]\) is a genuine map from
318: \(W\) to \(\mathbf P^1\), since its only common zero was removed.
319: Consequently this Cox/Hopf identification extends over all of \(W\),
320: including the inner link, with the original ordered branch marking.
321: 
322: The pullback of the source Picard line to
323: \(\mathbf P^1\times D_\rho\) has degree zero on every normalization
324: fibre and hence is a line from the parameter disc. Rigidifying it along
325: the zero section gives a literal trivialization. The source-line unit
326: in the suspension isotopy above is retained in this trivialization.
327: It contributes the same extending scalar gauge on both sides of
328: (SC.14). No degree-zero line or scalar winding is discarded.
329: 
330: The circle line also extends on the whole excision complement; this
331: does not follow just from constructing its restriction to the normal
332: sphere bundle. Put \(Z'= (Y\times D_\rho)\setminus\operatorname{int}B^7\).
333: It has \(H^2(Z';\mathbb Z)=H^3(Z';\mathbb Z)=0\). The codimension-three
334: Thom sequence of the properly embedded connected \(W\) therefore gives
335: \[
336:  H^2(Z'\setminus W;\mathbb Z)\xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }
337:              H^0(W;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z.             \tag{SC.15a}
338: \]
339: The isomorphism is evaluation on the oriented normal meridian. Choose
340: the line with meridian value one. On the outer complement the actual
341: Euler line has this value, and \(H^2\) of that complement is also
342: \(\mathbb Z\): each fibre is the complement of the unknotted \(S^2\)
343: in \(S^5\), with \(H^1=0,H^2=\mathbb Z\), and the oriented return
344: preserves its generator. The Wang sequence has no other degree-two
345: term. The ratio of the two lines is therefore topologically trivial;
346: multiplication by a chosen nonzero section of that ratio is the actual
347: boundary bundle isomorphism. It retains the physical circle action.
348: 
349: Furthermore restriction
350: \(H^2(S(\nu W))\to H^2(S(\nu W)|_{F\times S^1})\) is an isomorphism:
351: it is an isomorphism both on the base \(H^2\) and on the meridian
352: summand of the sphere-bundle sequence. Thus the restriction of the line
353: in (SC.15a) is exactly the line (SC.14), whose outer identification was
354: just given in Cox coordinates. In particular its determinant class on
355: \(W\) is zero. On the inner link it is the same oriented meridian line.
356: Possible boundary isomorphisms differ by circle-valued gauges; their
357: outer parameter winding is extended over the specified
358: \(D^3\times S^1\) cap below. This retains that winding rather than
359: assuming it vanishes in the excision complement.
360: 
361: ## SC.5. Excision, its zero-index correction, and the transfer sign
362: 
363: Take a small seven-ball around the local lifted germ inside
364: \(Z=Y\times D_\rho\); its boundary contains the inner embedded
365: three-sphere \(e\). Remove the normal disc bundle of \(W\) from the
366: remaining ambient cobordism. Attach the product surgery collar
367: \((D^3\times S^1)\times S^2\) on the outer side, as in K7, and the
368: \(D^4\times S^2\) collar on the inner side. The outer closed boundary
369: is the actual physical mapping torus \(P_f\) after fixed-sphere
370: replacement. The inner closed boundary is the surgery manifold
371: \(N_e\) of the actual embedded link, with the same meridian line.
372: The further boundary is the normal sphere bundle over
373: \[
374:  \overline W=W\cup(D^3\times S^1)\cup D^4
375:        =(S^2\times D^2)\cup(D^3\times S^1)
376:        =\partial(D^3\times D^2)\cong S^4.            \tag{SC.16}
377: \]
378: These are the literal boundary identifications from the construction;
379: the two caps are not exchanged by an unspecified change of marking.
380: 
381: The actual Euler line extends over both collars. On the outer boundary
382: its class is the positive meridian class, by (SC.14); every possible
383: boundary gauge extends over \(D^3\times S^1\), since restriction is
384: an isomorphism on \(H^1\). On the inner \(S^3\) there is no
385: \(H^1\) gauge obstruction. Both normal bundles extend after choosing
386: their actual boundary framings; any resulting normal Pontryagin class
387: is retained. The extra sphere-bundle boundary has index zero, since its
388: base is (SC.16), \(H^2(S^4)=0\), and \(\sigma(S^4)=0\). The sphere-
389: bundle formula cancels the normal Pontryagin class and gives this zero
390: without a normal-framing assumption.
391: 
392: The orientations are the same two reversals as K7. The complex
393: orientation on \(W\) has outer boundary \(F\times S^1_\rho\) and
394: inner boundary the negative of the complex-domain link. The outer
395: boundary of \(Y\times D_\rho\) is \(-P_f\), and the excised inner
396: seven-ball supplies \(-N_e\). The remaining normal-sphere boundary
397: has zero index. Hence
398: \[
399:                          A(P_f)=-A(N_e).
400: \]
401: The actual positive family is Jin's mapping torus for \(f^{-1}\), so
402: \[
403:                          A_{\rm Jin}(f)=A(N_e).      \tag{SC.17}
404: \]
405: 
406: For an actual Seifert embedding of the lifted link in its six-sphere,
407: repeat the identical normal-tube excision, cap its boundary by \(D^4\),
408: and use the complex normal two-plane plus the radial real line. The
409: meridian line on the surgery boundary is the one in (SC.14); on the
410: three-sphere a line or its gauge has no additional class. The sphere-
411: bundle formula gives
412: \[
413:  A(N_e)=\frac{e(\nu V)^2-\sigma(V)}8
414:                       =H(e),                        \tag{SC.18}
415: \]
416: where the last equality is Takase's corrected Haefliger formula, already
417: recorded in F5 and used in the direct F55 link computation.
418: 
419: There is no unrecorded mirror sign in this comparison. In the local
420: coordinates of F9 the circle-first orbit orientation is
